---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { LIMITS } from "../lib/api";
import { DRAND_RELAYS, QUICKNET } from "../lib/drand";
import { formatUtc } from "../lib/time";

const genesis = formatUtc(QUICKNET.genesis_time * 1000);
---

<Base
	title="How it works · timelock.dev"
	description="How timelock.dev encrypts messages to the future with drand quicknet and tlock: the scheme, the network, what the server sees, and the limitations."
>
	<div class="container narrow prose">
		<h1>How it works</h1>
		<p class="muted">
			timelock.dev is a thin interface to <strong>tlock</strong>, a timelock encryption scheme built on the <a href="https://drand.love/">drand</a>
			distributed randomness beacon. Nobody holds a key you need to trust us with, and nobody can open your message early, including us.
		</p>

		<h2 id="scheme">The idea in four steps</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>
				drand is a network run by the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/leagueofentropy/">League of Entropy</a>, a group of independent
				organizations. Every {QUICKNET.period} seconds it publishes a <em>round</em>: a threshold BLS signature over the round number. No single
				member can produce it alone.
			</li>
			<li>
				That signature doubles as a private key in an identity-based encryption scheme (Boneh–Franklin on the BLS12-381 curve), where the
				"identity" is the round number. Anyone can encrypt <em>to</em> a future round using only the network's public key.
			</li>
			<li>
				Your message is encrypted with a random key using <a href="https://age-encryption.org/v1">age</a> (ChaCha20-Poly1305), and that key is
				encrypted to the target round. The output is a normal age file with a <code>tlock</code> recipient line:
				<code>-&gt; tlock &lt;round&gt; &lt;chain hash&gt;</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				When the round arrives, drand publishes its signature. Anyone holding the ciphertext can fetch it, recover the key and read the message.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The construction is described in <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/189">tlock: Practical Timelock Encryption from Threshold BLS</a>
			(Gailly, Melissaris and Romailler, 2023). This site uses the drand team's <a href="https://github.com/drand/tlock-js">tlock-js</a>. Its output
			is interchangeable with <a href="https://github.com/drand/tlock">tlock</a> (Go) and <a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/tlock-rs">tlock-rs</a> / <a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/drand-rs">dee</a> (Rust).
		</p>

		<h2 id="network">The network: drand quicknet</h2>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th>Chain hash</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.hash}</code></td></tr>
					<tr><th>Public key</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.public_key}</code></td></tr>
					<tr><th>Scheme</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.schemeID}</code> (unchained, signatures on G1, RFC 9380 hashing)</td></tr>
					<tr><th>Period</th><td>{QUICKNET.period} seconds</td></tr>
					<tr><th>Genesis</th><td>{genesis} (round 1)</td></tr>
					<tr><th>Relays</th><td>{DRAND_RELAYS.map((r, i) => <><code>{r}</code>{i < DRAND_RELAYS.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>)}</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			Round <em>r</em> is published at <code>genesis + (r − 1) × {QUICKNET.period} s</code>. The chain parameters above are built into the site and
			API, and every signature fetched from a relay is verified against the public key. A relay can delay or withhold a round, but it can't forge
			one.
		</p>

		<h2 id="who-sees-what">Client-side vs server-side: who sees what</h2>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th></th><th>Website (browser)</th><th>JSON API / original endpoints</th><th>Local CLI</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th>Where encryption runs</th><td>In your browser</td><td>On our Cloudflare Worker</td><td>On your machine</td></tr>
					<tr><th>timelock.dev sees your message</th><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, in memory during the request</td><td>No</td></tr>
					<tr><th>timelock.dev sees the ciphertext</th><td>No</td><td>Yes, in memory during the request</td><td>No</td></tr>
					<tr><th>drand relays see</th><td>Your IP and the round you decrypt</td><td>Our Worker's request for the round</td><td>Your IP and the round</td></tr>
					<tr><th>Recorded in the feed</th><td>Event type, round, time (reported by your browser)</td><td>Event type, round, time</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			Encrypting needs no network at all: the browser has the quicknet public key built in. Decrypting fetches one public signature from a drand
			relay.
		</p>

		<h3 id="privacy">What we store</h3>
		<p>
			One row per event in a Cloudflare D1 database: <code>encrypt</code> or <code>decrypt</code>, website or API, the server time, the drand
			round and its unlock time. That's what the <a href="/feed">public feed</a> shows. We don't store or log plaintext, ciphertext, IP addresses,
			user agents or cookies, and the site has no analytics or third-party scripts. Like any site on Cloudflare, requests pass through Cloudflare's
			network, which processes connection data for security and abuse prevention.
		</p>

		<h2 id="cli">Doing it yourself</h2>
		<p>
			For anything important, encrypt locally. With <a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/drand-rs/releases">dee</a> (prebuilt binaries for Linux,
			macOS and Windows):
		</p>
		<pre><code># once: point dee at quicknet
dee remote add quicknet https://api.drand.sh/{QUICKNET.hash}

# encrypt for 30 days (also accepts a round number or a date like "2030-01-01 00:00:00")
dee crypt -a -r 30d message.txt > message.age

# decrypt once the round has passed
dee crypt --decrypt message.age</code></pre>
		<p>Files made this way decrypt on this site, and vice versa.</p>

		<h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
		<p>Read these before you lock up anything that matters.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>It depends on drand.</strong> Decryption needs drand to publish your round. If quicknet pauses, the message stays locked until it
				resumes. If the network is ever shut down before your unlock time, the message can never be decrypted. Quicknet has run since
				{genesis.slice(0, 10)}, but nobody can promise decades, so be careful with very long locks. We allow up to
				{LIMITS.maxUnlockSeconds / (365 * 86400)} years.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>There is no early decryption.</strong> Not for you, not for us, and not if you change your mind. There's no reset and no recovery.
				If you lose the ciphertext, the message is gone.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>It trusts a threshold of drand operators.</strong> The scheme holds as long as fewer than the threshold of League of Entropy nodes
				collude. If enough of them conspired, they could compute future signatures and decrypt early. See drand's
				<a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/#️-security-assumptions">security assumptions</a>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The server-side API sees your plaintext.</strong> We don't store or log it, but you have to take our word for that. The website
				and CLI don't have this problem.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The website trusts the code we serve.</strong> Browser-side encryption protects you from us storing your message, but not from a
				compromised version of this site serving malicious JavaScript. For high-stakes secrets, use a local CLI.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Round granularity.</strong> Unlock times snap up to the next {QUICKNET.period}-second round. Unlocking follows drand's clock, not
				your device's. A wrong system clock only changes the countdown you see.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>No authorship or secrecy of metadata.</strong> Anyone can encrypt anything to any round, so a ciphertext doesn't prove who wrote it
				or when. The unlock round and the approximate message size are visible to anyone holding the ciphertext.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Not post-quantum.</strong> The scheme relies on pairings over BLS12-381, which a large quantum computer would break.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The feed is best-effort.</strong> Website events are reported by browsers and can't be verified, so treat the counts as
				indicative.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Size limits.</strong> The JSON API accepts up to {LIMITS.plaintextBytes / 1024} KiB of plaintext and the original endpoints up to
				{LIMITS.legacyBodyBytes / 1024 / 1024} MiB. Use a local CLI for larger files.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="reading">Further reading</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/">drand: timelock encryption</a> and its <a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/#-use-cases">use cases</a></li>
			<li><a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/189">tlock: Practical Timelock Encryption from Threshold BLS</a> (paper)</li>
			<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/League_of_Entropy">League of Entropy</a> on Wikipedia</li>
			<li><a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/harnessing-office-chaos">Harnessing office chaos</a>: Cloudflare on randomness and timelock encryption</li>
			<li><a href="https://gwern.net/self-decrypting">Gwern: Time-lock encryption</a></li>
			<li><a href="https://people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/pubs/RSW96.pdf">Rivest, Shamir and Wagner: Time-lock puzzles and timed-release crypto</a> (1996)</li>
			<li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20231207001505/http://www.eecs.harvard.edu/~cat/tlc.pdf">Rabin and Thorpe: Time-lapse cryptography</a> (archived)</li>
			<li><a href="https://sarcophagus.io/">Sarcophagus</a>: a dead man's switch built on smart contracts</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</Base>
